**No, not as currently written.** I should have caught this before declaring it done — let me show the actual blocker.

Look at `pollArtifactChanges` in `src/subagent.ts:499-514`:

```js
for (const state of interactiveSubagentRegistry.values()) {
    if (state.status === "cancelled" || state.status === "exited" || state.status === "unknown") continue;
    ...
    if (last && (last.type === "done" || last.type === "error" || last.type === "cancelled")) {
        if (last.type === "cancelled") {
            state.status = "cancelled";
        } else {
            state.status = "exited";          // ← treats "done" as terminal
            if (last.exitCode !== undefined) state.exitCode = last.exitCode;
        }
    }
    ...
}
```

Three things break the follow-up scenario end-to-end:

1. **Status flips to `exited` after the first `done` event** — the comment at `interactive-tmux.ts:486-491` says "the artifact is the source of truth — if the artifact has a `done` event, the sub-agent is finished." That conflates "this turn is done" with "I'm gone." The child's `pi` process is still alive (REPL stays open, per the protocol at `interactive-tmux.ts:37`), but our registry says exited.

2. **The poll loop `continue`s on exited** (line 500) — so even if a second `done` event landed in the artifact, the poller wouldn't process it.

3. **`state.injected` is a one-shot** (line 538, 543, 576) — the inject path is gated to fire *at most once per sub-agent* (`!state.injected` then `state.injected = true`). So even if status was right, the LLM would only see the first turn's output injected, never the follow-up.

The right semantic is ternary: `running` (turn in progress) | `idle` (REPL open, awaiting next prompt — pane alive + last event is `done`) | `exited` (pane actually gone). And `injected` needs to reset on each new `done` event after the first.

My new tool's guard `state.status !== "running"` is also too strict — it should accept `idle` too.

So my previous answer was incomplete. To make follow-ups actually work, the change is bigger than I implied. Let me check with you before doing it:

